WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.440
So when we're talking about trust for AI we&nbsp;
hear about these five pillars, right. Awareness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.480
robustness, privacy, explainability, and&nbsp;
transparency. So, what is all of this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.000
You're right Aishwarya. There are you know,&nbsp;
at this point in time we usually talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.720
five different pillars, but keep in mind that&nbsp;
this is a fast evolving space. This field is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:29.840
changing rapidly. But at this point we&nbsp;
usually talk about fairness, robustness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:36.320
privacy, explainability, and transparency. Let's&nbsp;
maybe talk about each of them quickly. Fairness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:44.400
is probably obvious it is to make sure that the&nbsp;
models are not behaving in a biased way. Now it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.360
may actually start, the challenges may start way&nbsp;
before a model is built. It might be understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:56.240
if the data itself is biased. If it is how do you&nbsp;
deal with that? When you build a model how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:03.600
make sure that the model is not systematically&nbsp;
giving an advantage or a disadvantage to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.560
certain group. And the definition of the&nbsp;
group varies by industry, by use case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:15.040
it could be based on sensitive attributes like&nbsp;
age and gender and ethnicity, but may not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:23.360
limited to any of those. You want to make sure&nbsp;
that the system is not consistently favoring one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:31.520
over the other in an unfair way. Robustness, you&nbsp;
want to make sure that your models behave well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:40.400
in exceptional conditions. How do you make sure&nbsp;
that the model performance is good over time?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:49.600
What is happening with the effective data drift?&nbsp;
Or for example, in the context of the of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:56.480
pandemic, we know that customer behavior has&nbsp;
changed you know customer patterns have changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:03.280
customer touch points have changed. Is your&nbsp;
model still behaving as expected, or if it is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.280
can you at least have an understanding&nbsp;
of how the model behavior is changing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.840
how data is drifting, how accuracy is drifting,&nbsp;
etc. Privacy, can you make sure that the model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.160
the data, the model that is built off of&nbsp;
that model, the insights from that model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:27.440
they are all that the the model builder&nbsp;
owns and retains control of those insights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.960
And how do you do this not just as in terms&nbsp;
of consumption of the output of the model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:39.120
but across the life cycle. How do you make&nbsp;
sure that data protection rules are in place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:46.400
through the model building testing validation&nbsp;
and monitoring stages. Explainability is probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:52.160
pretty obvious. How can you explain the behavior&nbsp;
of a model. Why was someone approved for a loan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:03:01.280
why was someone rejected. When somebody applied&nbsp;
for a job and that person was selected but someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.800
with very similar qualifications applied that&nbsp;
person was rejected, can you explain the behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:14.320
to the end user or to a decision maker.&nbsp;
Transparency, you want to be able to inspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.360
everything about a model. Can you understand&nbsp;
all the facts surrounding the model. Who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.200
built it, what data is being used, what&nbsp;
algorithms, what packages are being used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:33.760
who approved it, who validated it. All of these&nbsp;
aspects of the model, facts about the model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.680
should be easily available. Just like you know,&nbsp;
you have you buy a food product and there is a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.320
there's a label on it, you know, it has the&nbsp;
nutritional facts, when was it manufactured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.960
where was it manufactured, all of that. Just&nbsp;
like that for a model, you should be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.680
get the facts of that model very quickly. So&nbsp;
these I would say are sort of the fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:04:00.880
pillars of Trustworthy AI. The challenge is&nbsp;
making sure these can be done in a systematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.560
way regardless of what tools are used to build&nbsp;
the models and where the models are deployed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.520
So John, in the recent past, we have seen that&nbsp;
as AI systems were new to a lot of organizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:19.120
organizations have very recently adopted such&nbsp;
large-scale AI applications or systems in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.480
their workflow. And that's where we started&nbsp;
seeing these side effects of AI, right. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.040
that's where we pinpointed that, hey, like these&nbsp;
were some of the aspects which we need to target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.360
to make sure that AI doesn't have an ill effect on&nbsp;
the community, or doesn't have an ill effect on it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.760
on an entire perspective. So when we see that&nbsp;
organizations are facing such challenges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.960
when they are seeing such like roadblocks&nbsp;
with respect to building trust for the AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:52.640
what is the recommended methodology on making sure&nbsp;
that building such AI systems, or like building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:58.800
such trusted AI systems is easily done throughout&nbsp;
different business units of the organization and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.320
doesn't surely, you know, it doesn't really&nbsp;
streamline to just one particular department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:10.240
or team. How can we make it a big thing and how&nbsp;
can an entire organization productionalize this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.960
streamlined work? So Aishwarya, you know, you're&nbsp;
talking about expanding this across our company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:22.400
sort of setting up this governance framework and&nbsp;
that was one of the patterns we talked about. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.480
companies may not start there, but they may start&nbsp;
with one of the other patterns we talked about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:33.600
which is let's start with assessment or building&nbsp;
out a new use case, a new application that follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:39.840
Trustworthy AI principles, but yeah some companies&nbsp;
may want to look at a top-down approach and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:45.760
and set up the governance framework taking&nbsp;
into account that there are multiple streams of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.280
data science and AI activities going&nbsp;
on concurrently. But in all of these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.520
you know, regardless of which approach you take,&nbsp;
I think three elements need to come together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.480
And I would say these these are&nbsp;
these three elements are technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:08.080
people, and process. Technology is probably&nbsp;
obvious, we need to have guardrails across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.240
each of the stages of the life cycle.&nbsp;
When you're working with data, how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:19.120
check for bias in the data, how do you correct&nbsp;
that. That's a guardrail at the data exploration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:25.360
time. When you're building the model you&nbsp;
need a guardrail in place for model building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:31.120
for checking the robustness of the model, for&nbsp;
providing an explanation in development time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:38.880
You need a guardrail which will allow you to go&nbsp;
into valid, through validation into deployment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.400
and you need an outermost guard where&nbsp;
you think of it as a one-time guardrail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:50.720
which can continue to do monitoring of your model&nbsp;
and look at how it is behaving against thresholds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:57.120
whether the thresholds are being breached, etc.&nbsp;
Now, so technology provides these guardrails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.160
for all of the different five pillars that we&nbsp;
talk about. Now technology in itself is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:08.320
sufficient that's why I was mentioning people and&nbsp;
process. People because you need a set of skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:15.360
to come together. It is not just data science&nbsp;
skills. The MLOps paradigm requires you to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:22.160
the operational skills come together with data&nbsp;
science skills. You might have risk and compliance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:28.800
expertise coming into the picture. You might have&nbsp;
business analysts and business stakeholders coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:35.600
into the picture, and so on. So the right level&nbsp;
of expertise, personas who are collaborating to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:41.600
achieve this common goal is important. And then&nbsp;
finally, process. In a process, that term process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.400
you know, people may not always like that, but&nbsp;
the reality is you need a set of best practices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:54.160
for each stage of the life cycle. Whether it&nbsp;
is coping and building, or it is validation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:02.560
or deployment or monitoring over time, you need&nbsp;
a set of best practices. So technology, people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:11.040
best practices coming together make it possible&nbsp;
to loot Trustworthy AI at scale and operationalize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:17.360
it. Great, thank you so much John, like&nbsp;
it was very insightful for me because to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:22.240
understand kind of the AI systems we build, from&nbsp;
understanding it from a data science perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:28.000
to how it can be productionized and run&nbsp;
successfully in these large organizations. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:34.160
very important that organizations are responsible&nbsp;
to the people who are using it, right. So it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:38.800
it was really insightful that we got to learn so&nbsp;
many different things from you. In the meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.200
I feel like there's a lot of other resources&nbsp;
which is available for us to dig deeper and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:48.960
learn about fairness, robustness, transparency,&nbsp;
privacy, and explainability. So everyone who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:54.160
watching this you can find the right resources&nbsp;
in the description below, and soon we'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:09:03.840
posting more series of videos talking deeper&nbsp;
into each of these pillars. Thank you so much.

